4. SECURE ADMIN PANEL

Create a separate, secure administrator dashboard.

The public website must not expose the admin dashboard or administrative controls to ordinary visitors.

Use secure administrator authentication. Never hardcode an administrator password or expose secret keys in frontend code.

The admin dashboard should include:

A. Dashboard Overview

- Total published photos
- Total categories
- Total photo views
- Recent uploads
- Quick upload button
- Recent administrative activity

B. Photo Management

Allow the administrator to:

- Upload photos from a phone or computer.
- Add a photo title.
- Add a detailed description.
- Select a category.
- Upload a thumbnail or choose an appropriate preview image.
- Preview a post before publishing.
- Publish a photo.
- Edit an existing photo.
- Unpublish a photo.
- Delete a photo.
- Search and manage existing posts.
- View upload status and publication dates.

C. Publishing Workflow

When the administrator uploads a photo and publishes it, the photo should appear on the public website automatically.

When the administrator edits a title or description, the changes should appear on the website.

When the administrator deletes a photo, it should disappear from the public gallery and its dedicated page.

Use a confirmation dialog before permanently deleting a photo.

If an upload fails, display a clear error message. Never report a successful upload unless the database and file storage operations have actually succeeded.

5. TITLE AND LONG DESCRIPTION SUPPORT

Each photo post must support:

- Photo title
- Main photo
- Optional thumbnail
- Category
- Detailed description
- Publication date
- Optional tags
- Optional alternative text for accessibility

The description field must support at least 10,000 words per post.

Use a proper multiline text editor or a rich-text editor that supports long descriptions.

Descriptions must be saved completely without unexpected truncation.

The public photo page should display the description in a readable layout with appropriate spacing, headings, and paragraphs.

Support Unicode text, although the public website interface must remain in English.

6. PERMANENT DATABASE AND PHOTO STORAGE

This is one of the most important requirements.

Do not store published posts only in browser localStorage, sessionStorage, temporary memory, or frontend JavaScript variables.

Use a persistent database and permanent file storage.

Recommended architecture:

- Frontend: React with TypeScript.
- Styling: Tailwind CSS or an equivalent modern responsive styling system.
- Backend and database: Supabase.
- Database: PostgreSQL.
- Image storage: Supabase Storage or another reliable persistent object-storage service.
- Authentication: Secure administrator authentication.

Create the required database tables and relationships.

Suggested photo table fields:

- id
- title
- description
- image_url
- thumbnail_url
- category_id
- tags
- status
- created_at
- updated_at
- published_at
- view_count

Create additional tables for categories and any other required features.

Configure database permissions and storage access securely.

Only authenticated, authorized administrators may upload, edit, publish, or delete content.

Public visitors should be able to view published photos without receiving administrator permissions.

The website must retrieve posts from the persistent database.

Permanent Storage Requirements

Published posts must remain available after:

- Refreshing the page
- Closing and reopening the browser
- Restarting a phone or computer
- Clearing browser cache
- Signing out of the admin panel
- Deploying a new frontend version

Photos and posts should remain available for years, provided the storage service and account remain active and the content is not deleted.

Do not claim that storage is guaranteed forever. Explain that permanent availability depends on the selected provider, account status, storage limits, billing, and backups.

When an administrator deletes a photo, remove the corresponding database record and stored image according to the configured deletion policy.

Handle failed uploads, database errors, and incomplete deletions safely.

7. THREE ADVERTISEMENT PLACEMENTS

Create exactly three configurable advertisement placements.

The administrator should be able to enable, disable, and update each advertisement independently.

Advertisement 1: Top Banner

Position: At the very top of the public website, above the main content.

Requirements:

- A responsive advertisement container.
- Support standard banner advertising code.
- Adapt to mobile and desktop screen sizes.
- Avoid covering the website logo or navigation.
- Include an optional advertisement label.

Advertisement 2: Photo Page Advertisement

Position: Directly below the main photo or below the photo-viewing content when a visitor opens a photo.

Requirements:

- Display an advertisement beneath the photo.
- Support responsive ad formats.
- Keep the photo title and description readable.
- Ensure the advertisement does not overlap the image or navigation.

Advertisement 3: Site-Wide Advertisement

Position: A configurable site-wide placement that can appear on all public pages.

Requirements:

- Allow the administrator to select a suitable location, such as a sidebar on desktop or a designated content area.
- Adapt the layout for mobile screens.
- Avoid covering photos, text, or navigation.
- Support a trusted advertising provider's approved code.

Advertisement Management

Add an Advertisement Settings section to the admin dashboard.

For each placement, provide:

- Placement name
- Enable or disable switch
- Advertisement code field
- Save settings button
- Preview option where safe and technically possible

Save advertisement settings in persistent storage.

Only trusted administrator accounts may change advertisement settings.

Never execute arbitrary advertisement scripts through an unsafe rendering method. Implement a secure integration method for approved advertising providers.

Do not hardcode advertising revenue or claim that advertisements will generate income automatically.

